 / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Pyramid of the Niches is the best-known building of El Tajín, the pre-Columbian city in Veracruz, Mexico.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pyramid-of-the-sun"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pyramid-of-the-sun.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pyramid of the Sun</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Pyramid of the Sun is the largest building at Teotihuacan and one of the largest pyramids of Mesoamerica, about 65 metres high, raised on the east side of the…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pyramid-texts"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pyramid-texts.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pyramid Texts</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Pyramid Texts are the oldest Egyptian religious writings, spells carved on the walls of the burial chambers of pyramids to help the king reach the afterlife.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pyrenees.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pyrenees</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Pyrenees are a mountain range separating the Iberian Peninsula from France.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pyrgi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pyrgi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pyrgi</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Pyrgi was the chief port of the Etruscan city of Caere, on the Tyrrhenian coast at modern Santa Severa, north-west of Rome.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pyrrhic-war"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pyrrhic-war.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pyrrhic War</span><span class="enc-type">War · 280–275 BCE</span><span class="enc-snip">The Pyrrhic War was fought from 280 to 275 BCE between Rome and Pyrrhus, king of Epirus, who came to Italy to help the Greek city of Tarentum.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pyrrho"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pyrrho.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pyrrho</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pyrrho of Elis was a Greek philosopher regarded as the founder of scepticism.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pyrrhus-of-epirus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pyrrhus-of-epirus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pyrrhus of Epirus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pyrrhus was a king of the Molossians and of Epirus, sheltered as an infant by the Taulantian king Glaukias.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pyrzyce"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pyrzyce.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pyrzyce</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Pyrzyce, German Pyritz, is a town in the West Pomeranian Voivodeship of north-western Poland, south-east of Szczecin.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pythagoras"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pythagoras.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pythagoras</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pythagoras was a Greek philosopher from Samos who, by tradition to escape the tyranny of Polycrates, settled at Croton in southern Italy around 530 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/pythagoreio"><span class="enc-name">Pythagoreio</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Pythagoreio is a small town on the south-eastern coast of Samos, Greece, on the site of the island's ancient capital.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pytheas"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pytheas.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pytheas</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pytheas was a Greek navigator and geographer from Massalia, modern Marseille, who made a voyage to north-western Europe around 325 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pythia"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pythia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pythia</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">The Pythia was the priestess of Apollo at Delphi who spoke the god's oracles.</span></a></li></ul>
